meta {
  name: 5 Block time off
  type: http
  seq: 5
}
post {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/calendar/blocks/{{providerEmail}}
  body: json
}
body:json {
  {
    "startUtc": "2026-09-21T00:00:00Z",
    "endUtc": "2026-09-24T00:00:00Z",
    "reason": "Away travelling",
    "force": false
  }
}
docs {
  Records a period the provider is unavailable.

  ⚠️ **A time RANGE, not a date** — which is the whole reason this exists. It replaces a whole-day
  `day_off` flag on a fake appointment, so an afternoon off used to cost the entire day. Multi-day is one
  long interval, not a run of days: the replaced implementation looped over days and wrote **nothing at
  all** when start and end fell on the same date.

  `endUtc` is **exclusive**, so whole days need the following midnight. Three days off from the 21st ends
  at the 24th's midnight — ending it on the 23rd would leave the 23rd bookable.

  ⚠️ **`409` by default when live sessions fall inside the range**, and the message names how many. Blocking
  over existing bookings silently would leave the provider believing they were free while customers still
  held sessions that had vanished from availability but from nobody's calendar. Send `"force": true` to
  block anyway — the sessions are deliberately **left standing**, and it is then the provider's job to move
  or cancel them. Use `4 Check what a block would strand` first.

  ⚠️ `reason` is the **provider's own note** and is never returned on a customer-facing route. It is also
  deliberately kept out of the audit event.

  `400` when the range does not end after it starts — **refused, never clamped**, because widening a
  provider's time off blocks hours they never chose. `403` unless `{email}` is your own claim.

  Verify with `1 Availability`: those hours disappear. The morning of a part-day block stays bookable,
  which is the behaviour the whole-day mechanism could not express.
}
